<!-- BNEsim vs Nomad Comparison Content -->
<div class="content-section">

  {% set nomadBest = providerB.codes | findCode("NOMTRAVEL20") %}

  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>BNEsim</strong> if you travel to the same handful of countries on an irregular schedule and want data that sits ready without a clock running while you wait. Pick <strong>Nomad</strong> if you're covering a lot of ground over months and want one big bundle that stretches across a year. Both are built around the same problem, data expiring before you actually use it, but they solve it in different ways.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: Per-Country No-Expiration vs. One Big Long-Validity Bundle</h2>
  <p>
    BNEsim sells per-country and per-region data packs, and most of them come in two versions: a cheaper one that expires 30 days after purchase, and a pricier version of the exact same pack that never expires. You can buy the no-expiration version for a country months before you land there and it just sits on your account, ready whenever you actually arrive. Nomad takes a different approach with its <strong>Global-EX</strong> line: instead of a per-country no-expiration option, it sells bigger bundles (10GB to 50GB) that stay valid for up to a year, with the per-GB price dropping as the bundle size goes up, down to roughly $2.58/GB on the largest tier versus around $5.15/GB on Nomad's standard Global plan.
  </p>
  <p>
    In practice: BNEsim fits someone who knows which specific countries they'll be in but not exactly when. Nomad's Global-EX fits someone who wants one bundle that covers a lot of destinations over a long stretch of time without buying a new eSIM every time they cross a border.
  </p>

