  </div>

  <h2>Discount Codes</h2>
  <div class="content-box content-box--tan">
    <ul>
      <li><strong>BNEsim:</strong> No code to apply. Discounts of up to 70% show up automatically as a struck-through sale price on BNEsim's own pricing pages. <a href="/bnesim/">Full details &rarr;</a></li>
      <li><strong>Nomad:</strong> {{ providerB.primaryCode }}, {{ nomadBest.discount | lower }}. <a href="/nomad/">Full code list &rarr;</a></li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:.75rem;margin-bottom:0;color:#63553F;">We checked BNEsim's homepage, pricing page, and coupon page directly and found no promo code advertised anywhere on the real site, so there's nothing to compare a code against there. Nomad's affiliate program doesn't give this site a code of our own either; {{ providerB.primaryCode }} is the best public code we've verified.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Is BNEsim or Nomad the Right Pick</h2>
  <div class="decision-grid">
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--primary">
      <h3>Go with BNEsim if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You visit the same country or two on an irregular schedule and don't want data expiring while you wait</li>
        <li>You'd rather pay with crypto or keep a running account balance than check out separately every trip</li>
        <li>You want to schedule a plan to start on a specific future date</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--secondary">
      <h3>Go with Nomad if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You're covering multiple countries over months, not returning to one spot repeatedly</li>
        <li>You want a working discount code today, not a price that's already baked in</li>
        <li>You want to hotspot a laptop or share data across devices without checking if it's allowed first</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two Things Worth Knowing Before You Decide</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>BNEsim's no-expiration option is per pack, not automatic.</strong> You have to choose the pricier no-expiration version of a given country pack at checkout; the standard version still expires in 30 days like most eSIMs on this site. Nomad's long-validity option works the other way: buy a Global-EX bundle and the up-to-a-year validity is just how that product line works, no extra tier to pick.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Nomad has a real discount code stack and BNEsim doesn't.</strong> BNEsim's pricing already reflects its sale price with nothing to type in at checkout, while Nomad's {{ providerB.primaryCode }} (or NOMCYBER20, same rate) takes another 20% off any plan including Global-EX. If a working code matters to you, that's a point in Nomad's favor regardless of which pricing structure otherwise fits your trip better.
  </p>
  <p>
    For device setup and eSIM basics that apply to both providers, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2>BNEsim vs Nomad FAQ</h2>
  <div class="content-faq">
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is cheaper, BNEsim or Nomad</h3>
      <p>BNEsim, on a straight per-GB basis for most single-country pricing, and its discount is already baked into the price. Nomad can close some of that gap with its {{ providerB.primaryCode }} code, and its Global-EX bundles get cheaper per GB the bigger they are.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does either one expire</h3>
      <p>Both offer a way around the usual 30-day clock. BNEsim sells a pricier no-expiration version of most of its per-country packs. Nomad's Global-EX bundles stay valid for up to a year by default, no separate tier needed.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does BNEsim or Nomad have a working discount code</h3>
      <p>Nomad does: {{ providerB.primaryCode }} or NOMCYBER20, both 20% off. BNEsim doesn't advertise a code anywhere on its real site; its discounts are already applied as a sale price.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which one is better for a digital nomad covering several countries</h3>
      <p>Nomad's Global-EX line is built specifically for that: one bundle, up to a year of validity, covering 200+ destinations. BNEsim works better for someone returning to the same one or two countries repeatedly rather than moving through many.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

